{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at 62d3fd2c6bfbed00603bdca1bf9c1ae15bf65bab. Artifacts: 45f88594 at internal/kernel, 0ea5a63a at internal/app, 18e63b7a at docs — all three standing at this exact commit.\n\nThird report against this promise. The second, fd21f83b, offered head 183ebe7d; codex requested changes at 8566d7d7 and I ratified that at 5f9fa04e.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. One new test in internal/kernel/backpressure_test.go, and nothing else. No production code moved: kernel.go, app.go and limits.md are byte-identical to 183ebe7d.\n\nTHE FINDING, WHICH WAS CORRECT. The governing request and limits.md both claim the capacity refusal is taken before the signed intent is parsed. TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeChaining observes only that the pre-append hook did not run, and that hook sits long after intent.Verify — so a guard moved to just after decoding would leave the suite green while making the documented claim false. The ordering was asserted in prose and unproven.\n\nTestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeParsing closes it. A request whose signed intent cannot be decoded separates the two orderings: at capacity it must be refused for capacity and wrap ErrBackPressure, whereas a parser running first would refuse it for being malformed, which does not wrap the sentinel. The test also submits the same malformed request with a slot free and requires a decode refusal there, so it cannot pass on a build where every refusal happens to wrap ErrBackPressure — without that half it would prove nothing.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, reproducing the reviewer's prediction exactly. I inserted intent.Verify before the capacity guard, the \"moved after decoding\" placement. TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeChaining stayed green; TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeParsing failed with \"invalid actor public key length, want ErrBackPressure — the capacity guard runs after the parser\". Mutation reverted, kernel.go verified clean, head unchanged.\n\nWHY I MISSED IT, since it bears on the four mutations I reported as thorough last time. All four attacked whether the bound exists: drop it at the app call site, disable the check, change the zero case, make the retry error anonymous. Existence and position are different properties, and a mutation suite built only from removals cannot catch a claim about sequence. Where documentation says \"before X\", the probe has to behave differently on each side of X. I reported those four as if they settled the conditions, and they settled all of them but this one.\n\nCONDITIONS PREVIOUSLY SETTLED BY THE REVIEW, restated so they are not re-litigated. Sharing ErrBackPressure between the capacity refusal and Submit's CAS exhaustion is appropriate, since both mean retryable sequencing contention and the messages and limits page distinguish the cheap refusal from post-work exhaustion; Rotate correctly keeps its anonymous exhaustion. The internal/app candidate resting on no predecessor is acceptable, because every live artifact at that path is stale and the candidate cites the governing request and performer promise instead.\n\nGATES at this exact head, uncached: internal/kernel 127.485s, internal/app 26.449s, -race on internal/kernel 165.468s, full go test ./... with zero failures, internal/docset 86.219s, go vet clean, go build clean, git diff --check clean, git merge-tree against main 27603774 clean.\n\nSUCCESSION. The three candidates at the rejected head are retired against their replacements, all effective, and all three replacements are non-stale. The shipped predecessors d97eed89, 82a33e46 and b930725a stay live for the merge to retire.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:45f88594c0937fdffb1803a6475dc7db4fbeaa1b","head":"62d3fd2c6bfbed00603bdca1bf9c1ae15bf65bab","status":"ready-for-review"}}